{
  "version": 1,
  "comment": [
    "Memory-API inventory for the memory-safety overlay passes. One entry per API,",
    "recording the ROLE of each argument so a consumer writes tag lookups instead of",
    "hard-coded argument indices. Sources: POSIX Issue 7, Linux man-pages (malloc(3),",
    "memcpy(3), read(2), ...), and the usage of these APIs in real C code bases such as FFmpeg.",
    "Deliberately omitted: wide-char and _s variants (wcscpy, memcpy_s, ...) and Win32 APIs",
    "(alloca is included, as the `stack` family). They surface in",
    "the coverage report as 'seen but not inventoried', which is how this list is meant to",
    "grow - an entry here needs its man-page argument roles, nothing else.",
    "Argument indices are 1-based. `untrustedCall` marks a call whose RESULT or variadic",
    "outputs carry untrusted data with no single buffer argument to point at (getenv, the",
    "scanf family). C++ new/delete are indistinguishable from new[]/delete[] in the graph",
    "(the frontend emits the same operator for both), so both carry the plain `new` family.",
    "Count-by-size allocators (calloc, reallocarray) declare both factors: `count` is the",
    "element-count role and `len` the byte-size role; both arguments carry the mem-len tag",
    "and the overflow question is asked about the product. A realloc is the",
    "third family, `realloc`: it invalidates its input pointer and returns a fresh",
    "allocation, so it carries neither the alloc nor the free tag.",
    "Clamp entries: the value records what the call establishes about its result - min (bounded above by each argument), max (bounded below), clip (arg2 below, arg3 above). FFMIN/FFMAX/av_clip are FFmpeg's clamping macros; a project's own MYMIN is an external-config entry in the same shape. Used by GuardPass for clamps the frontend left unexpanded.",
    "Nullable-return entries: `nullableReturn` marks an API whose result may be NULL - ",
    "allocation-family calls that return POINTERS declare it explicitly; the fd-returning family ",
    "(open, socket, accept) does NOT: an int fd's failure is -1, checked with `< 0`, and conflating ",
    "the two would turn a checked fd into a null-deref finding. The pass emits `nullable-return` on the ",
    "call for MS-NULL-001: a dereference of the result with no narrowing guard between call and use.",
    "The str*-search family reads through its HAYSTACK, which is argument 1 (`src: 1`), not the ",
    "needle/char, so passing a pointer to be searched counts as a read-through; `strlen` is a ",
    "reader of the same shape. These are the entries that decide whether handing a possibly-null ",
    "pointer to a call is a dereference.",
    "FORTIFY and builtin spellings: a `__builtin_X` call is the API X itself (same arguments), which the",
    "loader derives for every entry. The `_chk` wrappers the C library's _FORTIFY_SOURCE turns calls",
    "into (`__memcpy_chk`, and the compiler's `__builtin___memcpy_chk`) take an extra argument, the",
    "destination size the compiler worked out, and some move their other arguments: each has its own",
    "entry, with `api` naming the API its tags are valued with and `objectSize` that argument."
  ],
